      "Santería's roots lie in the Yoruba-speaking regions of southwestern Nigeria and the Kingdom of Dahomey (present-day Benin), from which hundreds of thousands of captives were forcibly transported to Cuba during the transatlantic slave trade, particularly between 1790 and 1870. Unlike earlier African populations in Cuba (Kongolese, who formed Palo Monte traditions), Yoruba arrivals—collectively labeled 'Lucumí' by Spanish colonizers—came relatively late but in concentrated waves, especially after the collapse of the Oyo Empire in the early 19th century. This demographic concentration allowed Lucumí slaves to reconstitute elements of Yoruba religious life within the oppressive constraints of plantation society and Spanish colonial law.",
