      "Santería has no written scripture comparable to the Bible or Quran. Instead, it transmits sacred knowledge orally through narrative (patakines or patakís), ritual performance, divination verses, praise songs (suyeres), and embodied practice. This orality reflects both African roots—Yoruba religious traditions were transmitted through apprenticeship and memorization—and historical necessity, as literacy was denied to enslaved Africans and written records risked exposure under slavery and later persecution. The absence of scripture has allowed Santería remarkable flexibility, as knowledge adapts through each transmission, yet it has also created challenges for standardization and authority claims.",
      "The primary textual corpus consists of the odu (signs) of the dilogun divination system. Unlike Ifá's 256 odu, dilogun uses sixteen cowrie shells to generate patterns from zero to sixteen, each with associated patakines—mythological stories about the orisha that serve as interpretive keys. When a diviner casts shells for a client, the resulting odu indicates which orisha is speaking and which patakín applies. For example, odu 5 (Oshé) is governed by Oshún and might invoke stories of jealousy, rivers, or wealth; odu 9 (Osa) is governed by Oyá and relates to transformation, cemeteries, or conflicts. The diviner must memorize dozens of patakines per odu and select the relevant one based on context. This requires years of training under a godparent, with knowledge considered proprietary to each rama.",
