      "Santería practice revolves around the ilé (house-temple), a private home transformed into sacred space through the presence of altars, consecrated objects, and ritual community. Each ilé is led by a santero/santera (initiated priest/priestess) who serves as padrino/madrina (godparent) to their ahijados (godchildren). The ilé functions as ritual center, divination clinic, initiatory space, and social network. Relationships within the ilé are structured by initiation seniority and orisha lineage: an elder initiated 20 years ago commands more authority than a recent initiate, regardless of age. This gerontocratic structure parallels Yoruba age-grade systems, though adapted to Cuban and diasporic contexts.",
