      "Cuba remains Santería's heartland, with an estimated 60–80% of the population engaging with the religion in some capacity, whether as initiated priests, clients receiving divination, or participants in festivals. Practice is most dense in Havana, Matanzas, and Santiago de Cuba, where cabildos, ilés, and public ceremonies are visible features of urban life. Post-1991 liberalization allowed state recognition: Santería is taught at the University of Havana's Department of Religious Studies, featured in official tourism promotions ('authentic Afro-Cuban culture'), and practiced openly by government officials. However, tension persists between state folkloric framing and practitioners' insistence on Santería as living religion, not museum artifact.",
